{
  "case_id": "2009-09-6338",
  "document_hash": "c2bb2e1bd033ec407fad0c2c82571c36787e3ba6b0808b6819ac519ef187ee14",
  "records": [
    {
      "attribution": "Court",
      "case_id": "2009-09-6338",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "c2bb2e1bd033ec407fad0c2c82571c36787e3ba6b0808b6819ac519ef187ee14",
      "evidence_id": "ev-c2bb2e1bd033-0000-0003",
      "kind": "procedural_event",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 1,
        "start": 1
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "The above-entitled matter came on for oral argument before the Supreme Court of the United States at 10:11 a.m."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Petitioner",
      "case_id": "2009-09-6338",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "c2bb2e1bd033ec407fad0c2c82571c36787e3ba6b0808b6819ac519ef187ee14",
      "evidence_id": "ev-c2bb2e1bd033-0000-0000",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 3,
        "start": 3
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "Believing its hands were tied by a policy statement created to prevent application of this Court's decision in Booker to section 3582(c) proceedings, the district court imposed a mandatory guideline sentence that exceeded the maximum authorized by the jury's verdict by more than six years. Sentencing Commission policy cannot override this Court's clear and unambiguous directive to courts to treat the guidelines as advisory in all cases moving forward, and any interpretation of section 3582(c) that permits the Commission to mandate sentences must be rejected, not only as a matter of statutory stare decisis, but because it would violate the Sixth Amendment."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Chief Justice Roberts",
      "case_id": "2009-09-6338",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "c2bb2e1bd033ec407fad0c2c82571c36787e3ba6b0808b6819ac519ef187ee14",
      "evidence_id": "ev-c2bb2e1bd033-0000-0001",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 4,
        "start": 4
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "CHIEF JUSTICE ROBERTS: What if the Sentencing Commission just issued a new guideline and said anyone with a -- a crack sentence, their sentence is going to be reduced by 10 percent? That wouldn't present any problems, would it?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Sotomayor",
      "case_id": "2009-09-6338",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "c2bb2e1bd033ec407fad0c2c82571c36787e3ba6b0808b6819ac519ef187ee14",
      "evidence_id": "ev-c2bb2e1bd033-0000-0002",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 7,
        "start": 7
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE SOTOMAYOR: What is the constitutional difference that would inure a Sixth Amendment right in the resentencing by the court that doesn't when it's a resentencing or a modification of a sentence by the Bureau of Prisons?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Scalia",
      "case_id": "2009-09-6338",
      "confidence": 0.99,
      "document_hash": "c2bb2e1bd033ec407fad0c2c82571c36787e3ba6b0808b6819ac519ef187ee14",
      "evidence_id": "ev-c2bb2e1bd033-0001-0000",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 21,
        "start": 21
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "It's -- it's -- it's not -- it's not section 3582 that\u2019s unconstitutional, and it isn't even the provision for being guided by a policy statement of the Sentencing Commission that's unconstitutional. It is the nature of the Sentencing Commission's policy statement that is unconstitutional."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Breyer",
      "case_id": "2009-09-6338",
      "confidence": 0.99,
      "document_hash": "c2bb2e1bd033ec407fad0c2c82571c36787e3ba6b0808b6819ac519ef187ee14",
      "evidence_id": "ev-c2bb2e1bd033-0001-0002",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 22,
        "start": 22
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "What\u2019s unconstitutional about that?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Ms. Kruger",
      "case_id": "2009-09-6338",
      "confidence": 0.99,
      "document_hash": "c2bb2e1bd033ec407fad0c2c82571c36787e3ba6b0808b6819ac519ef187ee14",
      "evidence_id": "ev-c2bb2e1bd033-0001-0003",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 27,
        "start": 27
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "The provisions of the Sentencing Reform Act at issue in this case, unlike the provisions that were at issue in Booker, do not govern the imposition of sentence. They instead provide a discretionary mechanism for the exercise of leniency for defendants who have already been sentenced."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Ginsburg",
      "case_id": "2009-09-6338",
      "confidence": 0.99,
      "document_hash": "c2bb2e1bd033ec407fad0c2c82571c36787e3ba6b0808b6819ac519ef187ee14",
      "evidence_id": "ev-c2bb2e1bd033-0001-0004",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 33,
        "start": 33
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE GINSBURG: Have there been other guidelines ranges that have been reduced, and, if so, were those also retroactive?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Ms. Kruger",
      "case_id": "2009-09-6338",
      "confidence": 0.99,
      "document_hash": "c2bb2e1bd033ec407fad0c2c82571c36787e3ba6b0808b6819ac519ef187ee14",
      "evidence_id": "ev-c2bb2e1bd033-0001-0005",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 33,
        "start": 33
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "There have -- there have been many guidelines ranges that have been reduced over the course of the existence of the sentencing guidelines. I believe that right now, the current number of -- of guidelines amendments the Sentencing Commission has made retroactive stands at 27."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Kennedy",
      "case_id": "2009-09-6338",
      "confidence": 0.99,
      "document_hash": "c2bb2e1bd033ec407fad0c2c82571c36787e3ba6b0808b6819ac519ef187ee14",
      "evidence_id": "ev-c2bb2e1bd033-0001-0006",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 38,
        "start": 38
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE KENNEDY: When the judge considers the adjustment motion under the -- under the section, does he consider how the prisoner has behaved in prison; i.e., if he has behaved very badly, he doesn't give the -- the reduction?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Ms. Kruger",
      "case_id": "2009-09-6338",
      "confidence": 0.99,
      "document_hash": "c2bb2e1bd033ec407fad0c2c82571c36787e3ba6b0808b6819ac519ef187ee14",
      "evidence_id": "ev-c2bb2e1bd033-0001-0007",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 38,
        "start": 38
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "The district court can consider any disciplinary proceedings that have occurred in the course of the defendant's imprisonment, yes."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Chief Justice Roberts",
      "case_id": "2009-09-6338",
      "confidence": 0.99,
      "document_hash": "c2bb2e1bd033ec407fad0c2c82571c36787e3ba6b0808b6819ac519ef187ee14",
      "evidence_id": "ev-c2bb2e1bd033-0001-0008",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 39,
        "start": 39
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "CHIEF JUSTICE ROBERTS: I\u2019m troubled by your response to Justice Kennedy's previous question, that the judge can take into account conduct in prison and all these other things. It does seem to open it up to other factors than the crack cocaine disparity, and once you\u2019re looking at other factors, why not look at everything?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Ms. Kruger",
      "case_id": "2009-09-6338",
      "confidence": 0.99,
      "document_hash": "c2bb2e1bd033ec407fad0c2c82571c36787e3ba6b0808b6819ac519ef187ee14",
      "evidence_id": "ev-c2bb2e1bd033-0001-0009",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 39,
        "start": 39
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "Well, I think the answer to that question is resolved by looking at the plain text of 3582(c)(2), Mr. Chief Justice, which directs that district courts have a discretion to reduce sentences in a manner that\u2019s consistent with applicable policy statements, but after considering these statutory sentencing factors under section 3553(a), which include, of course, the need to protect the public from future crimes committed by the defendant, as well as the history and characteristics of the prisoner."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Kennedy",
      "case_id": "2009-09-6338",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "c2bb2e1bd033ec407fad0c2c82571c36787e3ba6b0808b6819ac519ef187ee14",
      "evidence_id": "ev-c2bb2e1bd033-0002-0000",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 40,
        "start": 40
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "Does the Justice Department ever make recommendations that prisoners like this have their sentence commuted?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Kennedy",
      "case_id": "2009-09-6338",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "c2bb2e1bd033ec407fad0c2c82571c36787e3ba6b0808b6819ac519ef187ee14",
      "evidence_id": "ev-c2bb2e1bd033-0002-0001",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 41,
        "start": 41
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "there -- how many commutations last year? None. How many commutations the year before? Five."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Ms. Kruger",
      "case_id": "2009-09-6338",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "c2bb2e1bd033ec407fad0c2c82571c36787e3ba6b0808b6819ac519ef187ee14",
      "evidence_id": "ev-c2bb2e1bd033-0002-0007",
      "kind": "fact",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 41,
        "start": 41
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "I can tell you that the government very much takes those considerations into account when making recommendations about available sentence reductions under section 3582(c)(2)."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Stevens",
      "case_id": "2009-09-6338",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "c2bb2e1bd033ec407fad0c2c82571c36787e3ba6b0808b6819ac519ef187ee14",
      "evidence_id": "ev-c2bb2e1bd033-0002-0003",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 42,
        "start": 42
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "You think the statute would have prohibited a more generous policy statement, then?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Scalia",
      "case_id": "2009-09-6338",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "c2bb2e1bd033ec407fad0c2c82571c36787e3ba6b0808b6819ac519ef187ee14",
      "evidence_id": "ev-c2bb2e1bd033-0002-0004",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 43,
        "start": 43
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "But I -- I still don't understand how you fit into that your answer to the Chief Justice earlier that, in fact, the court can consider other factors in -- in 3553 when it's making the reduction."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Lisa B. Freeland",
      "case_id": "2009-09-6338",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "c2bb2e1bd033ec407fad0c2c82571c36787e3ba6b0808b6819ac519ef187ee14",
      "evidence_id": "ev-c2bb2e1bd033-0002-0005",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 45,
        "start": 45
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "A couple of points. I wanted to answer Justice Kennedy's question. The court is not only permitted to consider bad behavior in prison; under 1B1.10, the court is required. The word is \"shall.\""
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Chief Justice Roberts",
      "case_id": "2009-09-6338",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "c2bb2e1bd033ec407fad0c2c82571c36787e3ba6b0808b6819ac519ef187ee14",
      "evidence_id": "ev-c2bb2e1bd033-0002-0006",
      "kind": "procedural_event",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 45,
        "start": 45
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "would ask that the judgment of the court of appeals be affirmed."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Source extraction",
      "case_id": "2009-09-6338",
      "confidence": 0.0,
